  "answer_text": "How long sliced American cheese lasts depends entirely on whether you bought **freshly sliced cheese from the deli counter** or **pre-packaged, processed slices** (like Kraft Singles). \n\n---\n\n### 1. Fresh Deli-Sliced American Cheese\nBecause it is freshly sliced and typically has fewer preservatives than pre-packaged singles, its shelf life is shorter.\n* **Unopened (in original deli paper/bag):** It will last for **2 to 3 weeks** in the refrigerator from the day of purchase.\n* **Opened:** Once you open the deli bag, it is best consumed within **5 to 7 days** for peak quality and freshness.\n\n### 2. Pre-Packaged Processed Slices (e.g., Kraft Singles)\nThese slices are pasteurized, contain emulsifiers/preservatives, and are individually wrapped in plastic, which dramatically extends their shelf life.\n* **Unopened:** They can easily last in the refrigerator for **1 to 2 months past the \"best-by\" date** printed on the package. \n* **Opened (outer bag opened, but individual slices wrapped):** Even after the main outer bag is opened, the individually wrapped slices will remain good in the fridge for **1 to 2 months**, provided the outer bag is kept sealed or they are stored in a secondary airtight bag.\n\n---\n\n### How to Maximize Shelf Life\n* **Keep it cold:** Store the cheese in the main body of your refrigerator (ideally between 35\u00b0F and 40\u00b0F) rather than in the door, where temperatures fluctuate every time you open it. The deli drawer is the perfect spot.\n* **Seal it tightly:** If you bought deli-sliced cheese, once the original paper is opened, wrap the leftovers tightly in plastic wrap, aluminum foil, or place them in a zip-top freezer bag with the air squeezed out. Exposure to air will dry the cheese out and cause it to harden.\n* **Avoid touching with bare hands:** When grabbing a slice of deli cheese, try not to touch the remaining slices. The bacteria on your hands can transfer to the cheese and cause it to mold faster.\n\n---\n\n### Can You Freeze Sliced American Cheese?\n**Yes, but with a warning.** \nWhile you can freeze American cheese for **up to 8 months**, the freezing process alters its texture, making it crumbly and difficult to peel apart cleanly once thawed. \n* **Best practice:** If you do freeze it, plan to use the thawed slices for melting into cooked dishes\u2014like grilled cheese, macaroni and cheese, casseroles, or sauces\u2014where texture changes won't matter. Thaw it slowly in the refrigerator before using.\n\n---\n\n### Signs Your American Cheese Has Gone Bad\nDo a quick sensory check before eating if the cheese has been in the fridge for a while:\n1. **Mold:** If you see any spots of green, blue, or white mold, throw the entire package (for deli cheese) or the specific affected slices away. *Note: Unlike hard block cheeses, you should not cut mold off of soft, sliced, or processed cheeses, as mold spores spread easily through them.*\n2. **Smell:** Fresh American cheese should have a very mild, milky smell. If it smells sour, yeast-like, or sharp, it has spoiled.\n3. **Texture:** If the slices are slimy, excessively oily, or have developed a weird, hard, crusty texture, discard them.",
